From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 14:14:40 -0400
Subject: ui: a split's row in each pane's colour, the strip on top as pills,
 the rail on one line

The owner's look notes of 2026-10-20, in one patch because they share the
window background, the colour mixer and the tab views:

- The address row over a split (toolbar T41, splits J16): over each pane,
  that pane's page colour, exactly as wide as the pane; over the gap, a blend
  from one to the other, 6 DIP past each side, and the hairline under the row
  blends the same way. Stacked, the upper pane's colour. A pane with no colour
  yet leaves the window's ground, and the other part fades into it. The row's
  text and icons take the one colour that reads on both parts. It follows a
  resize, a swap, either pane navigating, dark pages and full screen; before,
  the whole row took the active pane's colour.
- The horizontal tab strip (tabs R49 to R53): every tab, the active one too,
  is a rounded pill like the sidebar's rows; a split is two pills 2 DIP apart,
  each half as wide as any other tab, so its titles are not cut to a few
  letters; the strip's controls sit on the ground with no tile or disc; the
  dividers, the toolbar's divider before the moon and the split button take
  the text colour, not Material blue.
- The rail's column (sidebar Y28): every glyph stands on the column's centre
  within half a DIP. An emoji is centred by where its picture falls, not by
  its advance (on the Mac an eighth of an em left of the line); a chord chip
  widens by an even number of DIP; Archived and downloads sit on the
  favicons' column in the open sidebar; the icons follow a density change.
  Every drawn pixel counts as ink (Segoe UI Emoji's edge is fainter than a
  quarter), and a glyph wider than its box is drawn unclipped in the real box,
  since RenderText rounds its centring toward zero.
- A read of a page's top edge begun before dark pages changed is dropped when
  it comes back (dark-pages Dk8): a tab just sent to the background could put
  its old pale edge back and show it when selected again. Older than this
  patch; its Windows run found it.

Tests: PageColourRowTest.ASplitRowTakesEachPanesColour and
PageBarColorSupplierTest.ASplitRowsForegroundReadsOnBothParts (the row),
HorizontalStripTest.* (the strip), SidebarColumnTest.EveryGlyphStandsOnTheColumn
and GlyphInkTest.* (the column), PageColourRowTest.AnEdgeReadBegunBeforeTheSwitchIsDropped
(the late read); each fails with its change taken out.

Why: the owner saw the split's row in one pane's colour, the strip on top
drawn badly, and the rail's emoji off the line of its "+".
Removable when: never; the look is Stedding's own.
